{"answers": [{"question_id": "Q1", "question": "What event or condition triggers the scheduling of a vendor meeting — for example, is it calendar-based, performance-threshold-based, or initiated ad hoc by a specific role?", "answer": "The meeting is initiated when an improvement request or equipment issue surfaces, prompting the process to begin. It is not described as strictly calendar-based or threshold-based; rather, it starts when a need for improvement is identified. The exact triggering condition beyond this is not specified in the available information."}, {"question_id": "Q2", "question": "Which internal roles are required to attend the vendor meeting, and is there a defined role responsible for facilitating or leading it?", "answer": "The technical specialist handling the equipment and a coordinator responsible for organizing logistics are the primary internal participants. The coordinator acts as the facilitator, keeping things structured during the session. A manager is also involved, though primarily after the meeting concludes. The vendor's representative attends from the external side."}, {"question_id": "Q3", "question": "How are discussion points and decisions captured during the meeting — for example, through free-form notes, a structured template, or a recording tool?", "answer": "The coordinator captures decisions and unresolved questions as the meeting progresses, storing them in a notes document. The specific format — whether free-form or templated — is not explicitly defined in the available information, but a dedicated notes artifact is used to collect what was agreed upon and what remains open."}, {"question_id": "Q4", "question": "Who is responsible for creating the post-meeting summary, and is there a standard format or destination system where it must be stored or sent?", "answer": "The meeting coordinator drafts and distributes the summary after the session ends. It is stored in a shared document space accessible to relevant participants. A rigid standard format is not specified, but the summary is distributed to stakeholders and kept in a centralized repository for reference."}, {"question_id": "Q5", "question": "How are action items assigned and tracked after the meeting — for example, are they entered into a task management tool, sent via email, or managed through a spreadsheet?", "answer": "A dedicated tracking tool is used to monitor follow-up tasks. The manager assigns responsibility and deadlines to each item, and the designated owners then update progress within that same system. Automated reminders and status updates are identified as an opportunity to improve this tracking step."}, {"question_id": "Q6", "question": "What criteria or event marks the workflow as fully complete — for example, when all action items are closed, when a sign-off is given by a specific role, or after a defined review period?", "answer": "The workflow is considered complete when all assigned follow-up tasks have been resolved and their progress confirmed. A formal sign-off role or defined review period is not explicitly mentioned. Closure is effectively tied to every action item reaching a finished state with no outstanding ownership gaps."}]}
```
